\title{The type of Spaces is Segal}
\tag{Changelog}
\author{samueltoth}
\date{2026-08-30}
\meta{source}{[PR\startverb#\stopverb218](https://github.com/samtoth/agda-synthetic-categories/pull/218)}
\meta{source}{[PR\startverb#\stopverb220](https://github.com/samtoth/agda-synthetic-categories/pull/220)}
\meta{source}{[PR\startverb#\stopverb225](https://github.com/samtoth/agda-synthetic-categories/pull/225)}
\import{stt-macros}


\p{This changelog details the work I have done recently on
cleaning up and extending the formalisation of the category of
spaces. The main result is kind of [directed univalence for covariant families over #{\Delta^2}](tot-L034)
, leading to a formalisation of the fact that the [classifier for
left fibrations](stt-00AS) is a [Segal type](stt-00AI) (\ref{tot-YE55}).}

\subtree{
\title{Exponentiating covariant families}

\p{Firstly, it cleans up a hole in the original formalisation, namely that #{X \to Y}
is covariant for contravariant families #{X} and covariant families
#{Y} (\ref{stt-00OM}). This conceptually isn't a challenging proof, but it was
difficult to manage all of the coherences. One of the ways this was made easier is
via the [idea of lifting problems at families of types](stt-00NX) which is a kind
of straightening-unstraightening trick. \ref{stt-00UJ} then gives us a nice
characterisation of lifts of #{(b \mapsto X_b \to Y_b)} against #{\{0\} \to \Delta^1}.
We provide two formalised proofs of the main fact, one which is quite nice and high
level, but uses the yet to be formalised fact that [left-anodyne maps](stt-00EB) are stable
under base-change by [right fibrations](stt-00OH). The second takes a slightly brute
force approach by constructing an equivalence by hand, using the co and
contravariant transport explicitly. Since this PR was made, the observation of
[[robschellingerhout]] that if each fibre of a type family is a groupoid, then
it is enough to give a section of the gap map (\ref{stt-00VJ}) has
been [formalised in the library](stt-00VI) by [Fredrik](fredrikbakke), which should
allow us to simplify this proof.}
}

\subtree{
\title{Equivalence of orthogonal families}

\p{A key lemma in the original proof of directed univalence \citek{GWB2024} says that
 a transformation #{\alpha} between families of spaces indexed by #{\Delta^1} is an
 equivalence iff #{\alpha(0)} and #{\alpha(1)} are both equvalences. It turns out
that this is the only part in the proof that uses the [cubes separate](stt-00J7) axiom. As
part of this work I refactored and generalised the (quite ugly) proof of this fact
that I had written in order to prove [directed univalence in the first place](stt-00KM).
Suppose that #{\{f_i\}} is a family of maps and #{\{X_j\}} a family of types which
each admit a point inclusion which is [anodyne](stt-00EB) with respect to the orthogonal
factorisation system generated by #{\{f_i\}}, then \ref{stt-00V7} now states that
a transformation between type families over some type #{B} which are orthogonal
to the #{f_i} is an equivalence iff the restriction to crisp elements of #{B} is
a #{\flat}-equivalence. When this lemma is instantiated with the left endpoint
inclusion of #{\Delta^1} and the family of cubes with their left endpoint inclusion,
we get back our original lemma, except now not explicitly using the type of spaces.
(note this also relies on the assumption that the only crisp elements of #{\Delta^1}
are the endpoints)
}

\p{Note that [cubes separate](stt-00J7) is not the only axiom we could take in order to prove
the lemma we need (and hence directed univalence). The weakest axiom we could use with
our current proof strategy would be (the slightly strange axiom) that the large class
 of all types with an initial object are a separating family. Cubes separate is
regarded as quite a strong axiom as for example, when modelling type theory in
presheaf categories it can be thought of as giving an 'upper bound' on
the representable objects. We have consequently wondered if we might be able to
prove directed univalence without this axiom, for example the usual model for
triangulated type theory satisfies the cubes separate axiom, but we may instead consider
a model in the classifying topos of distributive lattices (presheaves on
finitely presented distributive lattices), which does not satisfy cubes separate but
would satisfy the axiom that affine types (types which arise as the spectrum of a
f.p. #{\II}-algebra) separate.  }
}

\subtree{
\title{Disentangling directed univalence}

\p{One of the main ways that we have cleaned up the formalisation is by
  separating the construction of the classifier for left fibrations from the proof
of directed univalence.}

\p{For an arbitrary covariant family over #{B \times \Delta^1}, we may use
  covariant transport to give a pair of covariant families over #{B} together with
a fibrewise map between them. This latter bit data of data we call
[a covariant #{\Delta^1}-bundle](stt-00V1) over #{B}. We now reformulate directed univalence
as saying that this map is a [#{\flat}-equivalence](tot-AYGH). Once directed univalence is
proven for families over an arbitrary base, we may use the theorem of cohesive
type theory that [the large class of all types is a separating family](stt-00NM) to show
[directed univalence for the category of spaces](stt-00AT). An interesting observation from
this refactoring is that we may show that the transport map in the
definition of directed univalence has a section without any additional axioms.}

\p{The additional benefit of this approach is that we were then able to define
[glueing for horns](tot-R002) in terms of the regular directed glueing family, giving us
many nice properties almost for free. The main lemma from which we derive the Segal-ness
of the category of spaces is a similar kind of directed univalence statement, this time
for covariant families over #{\Delta^2}:}

\p{Given a covariant family over #{B \times \Delta^2}, we can construct
via covariant transport a triple of covariant type families over #{B}, together
with a pair of composable fibrewise maps between them. #{\Delta^2}-Directed univalence
then says that this map is a #{\flat}-equivalence. The inverse to this map is
given by taking covariant families #{X,Y,Z} together with maps #{\alpha \colon X \to Y}
and #{\beta \colon Y \to Z} and iterating the [directed glueing construction](stt-00UY) to obtain
##{Gl^2(\alpha,\beta) : (B \times \Delta^1) \times \Delta^1 \to \Type := Gl(Gl(\alpha), Z, \phi)}

and then restricting along the inclusion #{Delta^2 \to \Delta^1 \times \Delta^1}.
}
}

\p{With all of this infrastructure in hand, the proof that spaces are Segal
 follows easily:}

\transclude{tot-SYEC}

\subtree{
\title{Axiomatics}

\p{For the above proof, we require the following axioms:

\ol{
\li{There is a non-trivial bounded distributive lattice #{\II}}
\li{The interval is [directed]()}
\li{There is an ['amazing right adjoint']() to exponentiating by the interval}
\li{[cubes separate]()}
\li{The booleans cover #{\flat \Delta^1}}
\li{The three element types covers #{\flat \Delta^2}}
\li{#{\Delta^1} is Segal}
\li{#{\Delta^2} has thin hom-sets}
\li{The amazing right adjoints preserves pullbacks}
}
}

\p{Of these, we deem axioms 1-5 (or slight variations upon them) as essential, and
the remaining axioms should follow from the first set; however, this is yet to be
formalised. These have been tracked on the github issue tracker
[here](https://github.com/samtoth/agda-synthetic-categories/issues/256).}

\p{The characterisation of crisp elements of #{\Delta^2} should follow from the
characterisation for #{\Delta^1}. The fact that #{\Delta^1} is Segal (and a poset)
should follow from the quasicoherence axiom (which is a slight strengthening of the
directedness axiom) that we currently assume, and closure properties of reflective
subuniverses will give us the corresponding facts for #{\Delta^2} or even any other #{\Delta^n}.
Finally, the standard argument that right adjoints preserve limits should give us the
preservation of pullbacks for the amazing right adjoint, however, when attempting to
formalise this, I ran into some coherence problems that were difficult to just brute
force.}
}


